4375, 0.009918212890625, -0.021728515625, 0.006622314453125, -0.00748443603515625, 0.01187896728515625, 0.0016803741455078125, 0.01319122314453125, -0.0273284912109375, 0.019317626953125, 0.017242431640625, 0.0175933837890625, -0.00238800048828125, -0.0131378173828125, 0.00786590576171875, -0.01279449462890625, 0.03192138671875, -0.024658203125, -1.4185905456542969e-05, -0.00769805908203125, -0.0270538330078125, -0.00015878677368164062, -0.011077880859375, 0.0002541542053222656, -0.006805419921875, 0.04083251953125, 0.0187835693359375, 0.005367279052734375, -0.01641845703125, -0.00449371337890625, 0.01739501953125, 0.004337310791015625, 0.00891876220703125, -0.0022125244140625, -0.0224456787109375, 0.006622314453125, -0.022247314453125, -0.004241943359375, -0.00955963134765625, -0.0212554931640625, 0.0258941650390625, -0.04022216796875, 0.004055023193359375, -0.0304412841796875, -0.02008056640625, 0.01201629638671875, -0.0199127197265625, -0.01284027099609375, -0.009063720703125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Memory-based self-improving agents\u2014autonomous systems that learn from an online stream of tasks and improve over time by maintaining a textual memory bank of past experiences\u2014have shown great promise in recent literature. Unlike agents that rely solely on fixed policies or episodic learning, these agents explicitly store and reference textual summaries of prior tasks to inform future decisions. However, the reliability aspects of these methods, such as their consistency, robustness to changes in task order, and reproducibility of improvements, have been critically overlooked. Reliability is crucial for real-world deployment, where agents must perform consistently across varying conditions and task sequences.\n\nIn this work, we conduct a comprehensive re-evaluation of two representative memory-based methods, selected for their prevalence in recent benchmarks and their use of distinct memory construction strategies. We broaden the scope of evaluation along two axes: (1) including multiple runs (five per configuration) to quantify variance, and (2) randomly shuffling the tasks to investigate the effect of task order. These axes were chosen because, in practical deployments, agents may encounter tasks in unpredictable orders and must deliver stable performance across repeated uses. Our experiments are conducted in environments with complex dynamics and multi-step tasks, such as simulated household chores (e.g., \"set the table for dinner,\" \"clean the living room\") requiring sequential actions, handling of stochastic events (like objects being misplaced), and decision-making under ambiguous feedback (such as partial task completion signals).\n\nThrough these experiments, we make two observations that expose the fragility of current methods. First, agent evaluation is inherently noisy in complex environments and on multi-step tasks\u2014where complexity refers to environments with stochastic elements, long-term dependencies, or ambiguous feedback. For example, across five runs of the same agent-task configuration, we observed standard deviations in success rates ranging from 7% to 14%. Stacking a self-improving loop on top can further amplify this noise, with some runs showing performance swings of up to 20% depending on the memory trajectory. Second, the agent's improvement is highly dependent on task order: when task sequences were shuffled, average success rates dropped by 8\u201312% compared to the default curriculum order, highlighting the implicit curriculum effect present in prior work.\n\nTo better understand this fragility, we manually examined the agents' memory by reviewing the stored textual entries after each task. Our methodology involved coding each memory entry for completeness (whether all relevant steps were recorded), consistency (alignment with actual agent actions), and presence of omissions or contradictions. Two annotators independently reviewed the memory logs and resolved discrepancies through discussion. We identified patterns in what information is retained or omitted, and noted inconsistencies or gaps in the memory content.\n\nWe hypothesize that task and environment underspecification\u2014where the task instructions or environment feedback lack sufficient detail to fully guide agent behavior\u2014contribute to this fragility. For example, a task may not specify all necessary steps, or the environment may provide ambiguous signals about success or failure. To test this, we incorporated more detailed rubrics outlining explicit success criteria and added structured environment feedback (e.g., binary success/failure signals and stepwise progress indicators) into the memory construction process. These changes were operationalized by augmenting the agent's memory entries with rubric checklists and explicit feedback annotations after each task.\n\nThis intervention led to a measurable reduction in performance variance (standard deviation reduced by 30\u201340%) and improved average success rates by 10\u201315% (95% CI: 8\u201317%, p < 0.05) in our experiments, though significant gaps still remain, suggesting that other uncharacterized factors contribute to this fragility.\n\nLooking ahead, our work advocates for more rigorous evaluation protocols for self-improving agents, such as reporting results across multiple independent runs, testing under randomized task orders, and including stress tests in challenging, stochastic environments. We recommend that future benchmarks require variance reporting and curriculum-agnostic evaluation. Moreover, our findings on underspecification call for systems and interfaces that enable effective human oversight, such as tools for reviewing and editing agent memory, mechanisms for injecting clarifying information, and interactive dashboards for monitoring agent reasoning, to prevent agents from failing in unforeseeable ways."}}